#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf37b4ad-f1a1-5a13-8dd8-d35dc56030e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404379} {:answer/id #uuid "5980b150-496c-55d6-8d60-52afa76e9b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-11$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404380} {:answer/id #uuid "d5651eb6-a478-51ea-b582-8eee402be230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404381} {:answer/id #uuid "e56bac34-6636-58da-9694-caed75b4198b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404382} {:answer/id #uuid "08b74c80-69bd-5d43-8f58-45d6f43cbc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-11$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5651eb6-a478-51ea-b582-8eee402be230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404381} :answer-field/id #uuid "6009c72a-9f51-5ad2-ae87-7dc2fbcb6754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24be252f-8729-57e6-8082-158ca3588445" :question/math-academy-id "q-86724" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6x+{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region  $D={(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(3,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-2,0)$, and $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/75/752c84f5ab0fb122102629e9ceada21c1bba0b8f0e93b3fffe0639e4284badab.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(3,0)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}-6(-2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =17 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}-6(2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the point $(-2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues of a General 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19327b5d-0a87-5236-be0f-a4fc408fd3c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-2,-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404386} {:answer/id #uuid "576f8439-f4e9-50c2-87d9-f9f73bc72cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-2,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404387} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e67c97-48f5-50c6-a340-ea3827974147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-1,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404388} {:answer/id #uuid "f6484944-66bf-551b-bebc-dde27bbefb01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-2,-1,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404389} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d3b84a-b250-5e99-b0bf-b2e2d4abe21c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {2,-1,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6484944-66bf-551b-bebc-dde27bbefb01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-2,-1,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404389} :answer-field/id #uuid "e326b9f3-50f4-5bf4-9fe5-a7fbee316948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b75d1ac-303a-511c-86a2-e86c1eaa7201" :question/math-academy-id "q-86758" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$O=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,O-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nO-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(O-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(O-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1)+(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $2:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2\n$$\n\nLet's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}+2(1)^{2}-(1)-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the equation. So, we can factor $(\\lambda -1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65bec174-0235-5a32-a0d2-2892af4ddaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Events are Mutually Exclusive"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a7fb06d-1012-5494-b8ed-015056975eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\ne P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404393} {:answer/id #uuid "12b1de4e-720b-5103-b02a-93df60a43fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404394} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdbc724-6263-5eb1-9f31-2c494f2f3956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)<P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404395} {:answer/id #uuid "31a10f8b-ef54-5e35-b68f-279c0bb07e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404396} {:answer/id #uuid "88f3e987-8871-5966-8f22-ab50bf9c636c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\ne P(B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31a10f8b-ef54-5e35-b68f-279c0bb07e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404396} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ba2dd8-2220-580b-91e1-140112b524be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94a980d8-2b2e-5cf9-9a95-55c26c4f6102" :question/math-academy-id "q-87289" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\frac{1}{4},$ $P(B)=\\frac{3}{10},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{19}{40},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{19}{40} & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{10}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{19}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+12-19}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1fa984a-4005-5f18-91a1-e7be739a26f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404400} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a24b59-219c-5ab9-84d8-65b6683803d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404401} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a658d2-9bd0-5f27-8ae6-95960ef0e599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404402} {:answer/id #uuid "4508f230-b67f-58f6-9371-8d3f3d56004f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404403} {:answer/id #uuid "809b05b5-a1d9-5cff-895e-6d353eace3c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "809b05b5-a1d9-5cff-895e-6d353eace3c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404404} :answer-field/id #uuid "a831c2fd-e777-5d48-bdde-b20b03fa96ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e20e394-569b-5fe6-8d7e-276b93ef613e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87305" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{1}{15}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{5},$ find $P(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-1}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c64c1a-b7d5-51ae-b5d2-9a62f232cbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404407} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5066c8-e0c4-5b38-9465-2f4d0e3f690f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404408} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c58684-50d7-5ea8-90a7-fc01059630ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404409} {:answer/id #uuid "3584fd28-1d6f-517b-b3eb-c4379212f7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404410} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca51417-9fbb-52cf-9f57-772cce3140b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3584fd28-1d6f-517b-b3eb-c4379212f7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404410} :answer-field/id #uuid "2318f6dc-36b2-5ed9-8af3-6f105cfafa3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "610824ef-12b9-5ca4-a472-9a8e2ccc0745" :question/math-academy-id "q-87308" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{18}{25}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{4}{5},$ find $P(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{18}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-18}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac76d62e-6bea-515a-af06-e4fae7bc1338" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Exponential Functions with Different Exponents and a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "147b7050-1bb7-56bf-a406-2b9183de6cfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404413} {:answer/id #uuid "a45e44b9-d508-50d9-9942-ac9f170c45cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -3$ or $x\\ge 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404414} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd7a005-6153-5c6c-8264-dec69be6c819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404415} {:answer/id #uuid "eec96819-d9b8-55eb-bf60-48817f138fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404416} {:answer/id #uuid "1649e747-fd9d-5142-b2bd-bf10ded844c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 0$ or $x\\ge 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1649e747-fd9d-5142-b2bd-bf10ded844c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 0$ or $x\\ge 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404417} :answer-field/id #uuid "84492f7a-3823-5c2e-b8ff-3d045410e8f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "485dd702-22ef-5672-ba6d-aa433fac1c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-87772" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${3}^{4x}{x}^{2}\\ge {3}^{4x+1}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4x}{x}^{2} & \\ge {3}^{4x+1}x \\\\ {3}^{4x}{x}^{2}-{3}^{4x+1}x & \\ge 0 \\\\ {3}^{4x}({x}^{2}-3x) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {3}^{4x}x(x-3) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${3}^{4x}$ and get\n\n$$\nx(x-3)\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ The parabola is at or above the $x$-axis when $x\\le 0$ or $x\\ge 3.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bfb82543e7c05d0ca0b8f6c6973025400ad1253acbc57214173dbd3e8c37fef.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is  $x\\le 0$ or $x\\ge 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98dff91-dac6-54f4-874e-412ff82fc7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404420} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6b92be-72f3-51e8-a494-51777f66072a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404421} {:answer/id #uuid "1d09f0af-8d98-573b-a3ff-0be88001c588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404422} {:answer/id #uuid "12dbc12d-ca72-5f5e-816c-d12c14fc89e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404423} {:answer/id #uuid "fc401782-1079-528b-98fb-c84a43f43898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98dff91-dac6-54f4-874e-412ff82fc7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404420} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d269bb-4337-5bf6-b3aa-e727abe356a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "019afbc4-64db-5396-aa9e-b5972e6733b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-87862" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-{2}^{6x-1}<{12}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since ${12}^{x}$ is always positive and $-{2}^{6x-1}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n-{2}^{6x-1}<{12}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b27ef2-1d45-5405-8db2-6a0f32fffd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404427} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbabc41-ee20-588f-9fb5-7d7683297f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<3-{log}_{3}⁡(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404428} {:answer/id #uuid "20a1148d-a678-5339-a89f-18e31fc89eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<5-{log}_{6}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404429} {:answer/id #uuid "b669b97b-5f13-5c9a-b39a-fd990c4e287f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404430} {:answer/id #uuid "b42e9afb-b44e-5084-853e-ad95a5af9d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>6-{log}_{5}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23b27ef2-1d45-5405-8db2-6a0f32fffd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404427} :answer-field/id #uuid "2889b1bf-5c46-5be4-86d3-5d866d1cf34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5f64ef7-fc02-5b6d-a8c5-440eac71e226" :question/math-academy-id "q-87893" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x}+6>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x}+6 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x} & >-6 \\\\ {3}^{1-5x} & >-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{3}^{1-5x}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${3}^{1-5x}>-10$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7656d23-06c9-5d22-b62a-3636bca66ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Base or the Height of a Triangle Given Its Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef35ba9-a1a6-5901-a68b-21603e2051d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404434} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf3609b-ef5e-5b0b-9c1a-f2b156f94734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404435} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f39be9-b1e0-5587-868d-9f77d0afc794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404436} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9dd512-8f07-5391-be34-d4707734b6d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404437} {:answer/id #uuid "7c69f1fa-94c7-5dbc-8f48-c65d49727acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdf3609b-ef5e-5b0b-9c1a-f2b156f94734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404435} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d266bcc-fa01-524b-89f8-39a6f9df7aea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7bcc27a5-b987-5302-94f2-988d56293740" :question/math-academy-id "q-87910" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c19425664f79df1f46863f59268ebd27e20defce41b21e877926bbcadc9e5c3.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△CDE$ shown above. If the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=7\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{7\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 7CD & =84 \\\\ CD & =12\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/00/0014faf05eaf15d70f885802e5b820b0c72d42a96f400442f827ed229de8b240.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 12}{2} \\\\  & =30{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "479e5736-efe1-5e94-929f-3dcd6d13a733" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Exponential Functions with the Same Exponents and a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4466cd39-9e73-567d-823b-c399bff02272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404441} {:answer/id #uuid "3172eec7-1b0f-5cfa-83f3-1e0bdcb73644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}<x<2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404442} {:answer/id #uuid "b5687ebc-d415-5e04-8b82-d0d23c606f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-2\\sqrt{3}$ or $x>2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404443} {:answer/id #uuid "7254dc37-da09-582b-9081-9f0dd6e6a378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-2$ or $x>2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404444} {:answer/id #uuid "29d347e2-48fa-5c3d-9ae5-0c4b2283a50f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-\\sqrt{3}$ or $x>\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5687ebc-d415-5e04-8b82-d0d23c606f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-2\\sqrt{3}$ or $x>2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404443} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2157a1d-8075-5da7-8704-cf68a742b357" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99779233-4022-57ea-8b13-fcf906c4aeff" :question/math-academy-id "q-88308" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-{9}^{2x-4}{x}^{2}<-5\\cdot {9}^{2x-4}-7\\cdot {9}^{2x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{9}^{2x-4}{x}^{2} & <-5\\cdot {9}^{2x-4}-7\\cdot {9}^{2x-4} \\\\ -{9}^{2x-4}{x}^{2}+5\\cdot {9}^{2x-4}+7\\cdot {9}^{2x-4} & <0 \\\\ (-{x}^{2}+5+7){9}^{2x-4} & <0 \\\\ (12-{x}^{2}){9}^{2x-4} & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${9}^{2x-4}$ and solve the resulting inequality using familiar techniques:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-{x}^{2} & <0 \\\\ -{x}^{2} & <-12 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >12 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{12} \\\\ |x| & >2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to the inequality above is $x<-2\\sqrt{3}$ or $x>2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e77e8eb-4f76-565b-b3a2-5c91767ba39f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404448} {:answer/id #uuid "de87823a-03c0-572e-9778-3d0e131e7ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404449} {:answer/id #uuid "edee0d23-5e90-53e9-9a9b-3e624f83441e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404450} {:answer/id #uuid "45a9656a-bcf9-5ffd-9994-d663146b2ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404451} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c913bf-1bbb-5e26-96bd-2c1ec496da34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de87823a-03c0-572e-9778-3d0e131e7ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404449} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dec29b5-6835-594f-89d3-a76d54e5866b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe1794bb-4b33-510b-8172-929bfa639d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-8854" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+16x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\,=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-8\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+8\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ -8\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2}$, $\\beta =0.$\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "857d696d-6a3f-5fff-a5e1-b05148f28f55" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Sentences Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b5b70db-2b05-51ba-84eb-8426d1e48801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is romantic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404455} {:answer/id #uuid "203b1139-b63c-55f9-93a5-2e112bba5b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is romantic but not comic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404456} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd3bdcc-15fc-5268-bfaf-aeee4a0fee8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is comic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404457} {:answer/id #uuid "541680c1-2b24-55c6-8afc-6a4bedb19ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is romantic or comic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404458} {:answer/id #uuid "7aecce06-d66d-5e1f-8e08-ac26cc73bd78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is romantic and comic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "541680c1-2b24-55c6-8afc-6a4bedb19ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is romantic or comic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404458} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ec9b14-67d1-5d44-aaf5-9a87460b11fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0026e046-e84c-5c78-8cfa-12a3749b6a2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-89252" :question/problem "Simplify the sentence \"The movie is romantic, or the movie is romantic or comic.\"" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the phrase \"the movie is romantic or comic\" as the equivalent phrase \"the movie is romantic or the movie is comic.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The movie is romantic, or the movie is romantic or the movie is comic.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The movie is romantic or the movie is romantic, or the movie is comic.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"The movie is romantic or the movie is romantic\" to just \"The movie is romantic.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The movie is romantic, or the movie is comic.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely as\n\n\"The movie is romantic or comic.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4b6a5f-9aed-5dad-9356-4038bf9c0acf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse Laplace Transform Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48531a52-74be-58a5-ad7b-ff876a370cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=2{e}^{2t}+3{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404462} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9edfb3-bb01-53ba-acc4-d8149555d614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404463} {:answer/id #uuid "82f55f6a-fc56-52cb-9c7f-8a3c3e42bcc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=10{t}^{2}+{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404464} {:answer/id #uuid "3971cac1-f8e8-5bb8-bd17-d8d02ea2b90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404465} {:answer/id #uuid "53069da8-16e0-5ffb-a9a6-f7574465ddca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3971cac1-f8e8-5bb8-bd17-d8d02ea2b90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404465} :answer-field/id #uuid "aea651d3-f2f0-5676-bbf4-bf4713440432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c32439a8-2f0a-52f2-ba4c-04ee07df7da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-89821" :question/problem "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\frac{10}{(s-2)(s+3)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{(s-2)(s+3)}=\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{2}{s+3}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition property of the Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{2}{s+3}} & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-2}}-2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{10}{(s-2)(s+3)}$ is $f(t)=2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-3t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42525832-e186-538f-baf8-bb4418e9820b" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Inequalities Presented in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7c2a5d4-298c-5631-a9d7-aba5b729e118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y<3 \\\\ y>2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404469} {:answer/id #uuid "01bc8bf9-9741-5be3-bf6c-963968a707d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y\\le 3 \\\\ y\\ge 2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404470} {:answer/id #uuid "da29e297-529e-5fe2-b38a-d2962daba4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y<2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404471} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5ca256-25d0-5c51-b2d0-61bafb216142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y<3 \\\\ y<2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404472} {:answer/id #uuid "57ca1c9c-b7c8-5c26-8e55-f2e99026a45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57ca1c9c-b7c8-5c26-8e55-f2e99026a45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404473} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5be21f-60e7-597b-83dc-3c20339aa469" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "262609c0-5557-5285-b43c-f82fdb8825a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-90054" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8c1d5902a76a68e5f8358d4c3668e1be184ec7241184df4ff1363d41995f3aa.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $x+y=3.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-x+3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\"  inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x+3,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+y>3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=2x.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict.  Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbd52408-5f2f-58c9-9a88-bd992fe8752c" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Strict Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57e3339b-5c2d-5e02-8e56-922ef2cb88bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404476} {:answer/id #uuid "b2167803-87c2-5c4e-aa85-d576aac9cc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404477} {:answer/id #uuid "aa93daa1-24f9-58db-9545-6bf0487628c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>4x+3 \\\\ y\\ge \\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404478} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf061e8-e3ee-5abf-a78a-299067a79064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>4x+3 \\\\ y<\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404479} {:answer/id #uuid "6051d13a-7c7c-5804-88c5-53711a5cb6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2167803-87c2-5c4e-aa85-d576aac9cc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404477} :answer-field/id #uuid "94914b35-e16f-5398-9a50-4baddb153e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66b0d8c5-2487-53cc-b840-b06edcc12d56" :question/math-academy-id "q-90323" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e863789ff989ee1cb3d3fe6c22fe0d4daa1dac76d6fbfbebdad801006ca4c969.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=4x+3.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<4x+3.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{4}x-2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>\\frac{1}{4}x-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a258070e-5c3c-595e-81d6-3e0f9b9a5daf" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Mixed Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daea2326-2376-5164-b217-f74ca9ead6c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404483} {:answer/id #uuid "8b73faca-4331-5468-aa5b-5f3ece1295f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\le -x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404484} {:answer/id #uuid "83a1c4b0-cc77-5dcc-8de0-37f06af0e319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404485} {:answer/id #uuid "b45d0b54-a0ec-5724-b49e-549e13b6d4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge x+2 \\\\ y<-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404486} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2a88ff-6f4a-5ac9-a039-72c31f12f559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83a1c4b0-cc77-5dcc-8de0-37f06af0e319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404485} :answer-field/id #uuid "f86fe5f2-d23c-5598-bb24-07f9f597ec1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e5e2c49-2afd-5324-972b-43974f8bfdd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-90327" :question/problem "![](images/76/762e53967ddb3ccddfa1c9b7a2aa303d66fc1b761c9be8f409c0b91ecb0201de.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities represents the shaded region shown in the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ea3ae4-022f-5830-abec-118a76a06fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404490} {:answer/id #uuid "536feed6-9dfe-5307-aec5-0edb198d16a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404491} {:answer/id #uuid "20fcb72e-c053-5056-a323-074281dda331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404492} {:answer/id #uuid "51bc6cbd-e4d3-52a7-a0e3-484410623715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404493} {:answer/id #uuid "e96fb37a-ec85-52be-af50-069c63122601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20fcb72e-c053-5056-a323-074281dda331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404492} :answer-field/id #uuid "210542c5-de8c-5b63-a6a2-7bc9744e8618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fa44e0d-f47d-55dc-9787-703e1dcaa8bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-90493" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$\nII. $2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$\nIII. $2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4)=50{y}^{4}{e}^{x}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y)=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$ gives an exact equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67cf3cc3-3635-5acb-a7ed-452bb9111c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404497} {:answer/id #uuid "45d32fb4-03c1-5310-b998-78f449edc9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404498} {:answer/id #uuid "497b8fa1-01e8-5353-b89a-f5299641c340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404499} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb2a7c0-0bcf-56f1-9412-5bbd52e46ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404500} {:answer/id #uuid "52739661-1a40-5904-b3b8-993cc50b384a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67cf3cc3-3635-5acb-a7ed-452bb9111c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404497} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa88942a-1208-5ce3-a956-2912d19d6e95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "056f268e-6f23-5cca-ba50-28679fda2fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-90580" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\nI. $f(x)=7{x}^{7}$\nII. $g(x,y)=x+y$\nIII. $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z-2xyz+x{z}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is homogeneous of degree $7.$  It is a polynomial of degree $7,$ with no terms of degree other than $7.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $1.$ It is a polynomial of degree $1,$ and all of its terms are of degree $1.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is  homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c59fbbc-2dc0-562b-b432-ce3c7f59dad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ or $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404504} {:answer/id #uuid "c01408ce-3680-5eb5-8582-5d15eb08699d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404505} {:answer/id #uuid "0e45d023-bccd-58f6-9704-f559be5f8da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404506} {:answer/id #uuid "1b463397-33ab-5e64-907b-e8904f8a0935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404507} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e036bd-fcc3-5ff2-9e88-b80ab4086884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6e036bd-fcc3-5ff2-9e88-b80ab4086884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404508} :answer-field/id #uuid "117667a9-4c0a-559e-becf-2c255a4fa945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8bfd769-670f-5f6b-bec7-13134d36de0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-90706" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea90192a-98aa-5857-a246-925646efadb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404511} {:answer/id #uuid "58fb9126-dcca-5a22-be00-7d49e5cf7948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404512} {:answer/id #uuid "dad7dae3-861a-5560-878f-de18703b5976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-5x}-{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404513} {:answer/id #uuid "28ac766a-b6d1-5aa1-a97b-4a4386277974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404514} {:answer/id #uuid "9709ebe4-8c4a-5c48-8164-358a57e70e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-5x}+3{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58fb9126-dcca-5a22-be00-7d49e5cf7948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404512} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1363158-fb5d-52a2-aee6-d038f5cfedc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec0270d1-70c2-5347-87eb-345d880dbd64" :question/math-academy-id "q-90725" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+15y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+15{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-5.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{5x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9183d1-23e9-5f90-a209-d6b19d245c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404518} {:answer/id #uuid "226f3fd1-f941-5569-80fa-d157db347e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404519} {:answer/id #uuid "3b46698e-ccc1-5f9f-8f8c-b005abd4b4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404520} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4bce52-0e47-55a1-ac84-7a9f035eef89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404521} {:answer/id #uuid "38987e9b-2b2d-5194-a0c2-03820aac76b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "226f3fd1-f941-5569-80fa-d157db347e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404519} :answer-field/id #uuid "7222ce8b-9f86-5907-ac8e-304c68fbadee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc8df3f8-8c99-53c4-bedf-bdbc4a64bd8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-91157" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -13 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above ${a}_{33}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -13 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73f5eb09-e668-558b-b5c4-5f72bb86b5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-8{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404525} {:answer/id #uuid "b30692bb-6bff-5975-b521-cac176cbe736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+8{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404526} {:answer/id #uuid "0481513e-3cd0-504e-8e8e-90f5fc5b8b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-8{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404527} {:answer/id #uuid "7e64580b-23e3-5c32-bb1a-f2f9628a7159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+8{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404528} {:answer/id #uuid "85047f08-97d3-5876-b80f-412bddff211b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-8{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-7{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73f5eb09-e668-558b-b5c4-5f72bb86b5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-8{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404525} :answer-field/id #uuid "6844cde6-2278-591a-a355-02a1ecec361e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26e3c3e9-0f2f-55d8-b677-4d51648ddadf" :question/math-academy-id "q-91159" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -10 & -11 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above and below ${a}_{21}=-1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-7{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -10 & -11 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-8{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -10 & -11 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-8{R}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f91cfd00-78b8-5e81-b3da-fb66e4a255b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404531} {:answer/id #uuid "ba816428-2269-5d70-a23d-de4d9459d350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404532} {:answer/id #uuid "6b221426-9041-561c-a939-a652454c20be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404533} {:answer/id #uuid "7377369a-9c28-53fe-95a8-20a033d61460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404534} {:answer/id #uuid "4d223b12-1545-5649-89b4-f8afd0248272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba816428-2269-5d70-a23d-de4d9459d350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404532} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ebe645c-97a3-5257-bc32-0836ea3ff9c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "200a95d9-6cef-55b2-a6a6-5c194d5765aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-91163" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above and below ${a}_{23}=-3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f1ec99-e166-5986-a07d-f07be0dfeb31" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "367d5c7f-38e0-520e-83c3-49e14d8a0120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404538} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd078f9-4afb-5657-b580-57e60ff58958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-3{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404539} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eefb87-41c8-5096-b981-c10f00b1b3e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404540} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf61f67-9290-5f2d-a040-b0ae165dd343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404541} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fa6549-8cf1-506e-bea9-e621c1c2285b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdf61f67-9290-5f2d-a040-b0ae165dd343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404541} :answer-field/id #uuid "9df35bbe-f55e-5fca-b1fb-3fabd0065101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe1d7acc-f95f-567e-b05e-9ad830eda4f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-91177" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -29 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 7 & -15 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 9 & -11 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\nI. ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1}$\nII. ${R}_{3}:=…$\nIII. ${R}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries below ${a}_{13}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -29 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -29 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 7 & -15 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -29 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 7 & -15 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 9 & -11 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "958cdcf3-aaba-5275-85f8-9531f10b3d0e" :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5e36b83-15a1-5e52-9e6e-214c301a2528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-{C}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404545} {:answer/id #uuid "dd76300c-564c-55b8-901b-9e2bd1390ad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-3{C}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404546} {:answer/id #uuid "49b5e613-99b1-573d-91c5-9e3ef4f411b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-2{C}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404547} {:answer/id #uuid "0aca2d4a-7507-5511-8293-17a03410d0d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+2{C}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404548} {:answer/id #uuid "eeae7dbd-76b9-5ceb-9da7-1df8844c0123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+{C}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49b5e613-99b1-573d-91c5-9e3ef4f411b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-2{C}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404547} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e62e048-fd08-5b04-8d22-e746ab71041b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9ef8afb-c422-57bc-a155-4bf0fc567ca9" :question/math-academy-id "q-91212" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -16 & 8 & -16 & -20 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -17 & -24 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\nI. ${C}_{1}:=…$\nII. ${C}_{3}:=…$\nIII. ${C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-3{C}_{2}$\n\neach of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{12}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -16 & 8 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -12 & 9 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -16 & 8 & -16 & 4 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -17 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-3{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -16 & 8 & -16 & -20 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -17 & -24 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-2{C}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d059c56-ae7f-5fb6-8bc6-2ce97bb7f876" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df12665-ee8e-589e-bd84-ddf20099fe58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404552} {:answer/id #uuid "d09c688e-d77f-5dc2-a795-7353b6d5cf30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404553} {:answer/id #uuid "996332e4-ee9d-5bd3-bfe0-10173565755e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404554} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7008c7-4ccb-5f75-80d4-933508b111d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404555} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceb2712-9786-5f37-ba7a-d303316974c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996332e4-ee9d-5bd3-bfe0-10173565755e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404554} :answer-field/id #uuid "207a57e3-40d0-5996-94ed-9587a6c43801" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "deb7d641-51be-5b64-b25b-f4aefccbd21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93099" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=-4\\sqrt[6]{5x-11}-8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-4\\sqrt[6]{5x-11}-8=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\sqrt[6]{5x-11}-8 & =0 \\\\ -4\\sqrt[6]{5x-11} & =8 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{5x-11} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b9aca3-96f6-56d8-bf46-91df9e2cee46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404559} {:answer/id #uuid "c467d5d8-892e-5503-84ae-3f3235dba85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-3x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404560} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1ec65d-9105-5c41-abc1-a34560fa51bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404561} {:answer/id #uuid "045fd68a-57ca-585a-9e45-01698b432866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404562} {:answer/id #uuid "e496e4d3-4727-5c25-b373-87c737214851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc1ec65d-9105-5c41-abc1-a34560fa51bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404561} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bc90dc2-e777-5193-ab45-30485587cbf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "667003ae-52b1-572c-b234-cf239bc6b6d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-93675" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=12{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $12{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+4(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (4-8+8)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e93b6fb8-da4a-5b35-bd94-7c7b17d89869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404566} {:answer/id #uuid "694feab2-1865-543c-a32e-99cc6da36cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404567} {:answer/id #uuid "361fdeae-a507-5b99-bc39-5eed03c84e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-5x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404568} {:answer/id #uuid "34e11367-aa02-59ea-b0f7-6de755b9c9af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-5{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404569} {:answer/id #uuid "cf037a78-f8ae-5aad-9516-009441200fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf037a78-f8ae-5aad-9516-009441200fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404570} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a0bc794-25e1-51cf-8047-1c1bc0cb1fb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b0d7eeb-30c2-58fb-b039-5edb4ff9136a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93678" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=10{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}-3(\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x})-4(\\alpha x{e}^{-x}) & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ (x-2-3+3x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x{e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66d7c870-ba49-56c9-a245-3b658ebd3c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404573} {:answer/id #uuid "0bae63c5-52a4-5804-81fa-eb1b683e4f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404574} {:answer/id #uuid "06f5a036-10ff-5195-9453-9491663dcba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404575} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4a8825-cfc2-5cbd-a587-a43b98c45b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-3x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404576} {:answer/id #uuid "1514ca83-9567-51c1-b1f9-3a73252293f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bae63c5-52a4-5804-81fa-eb1b683e4f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404574} :answer-field/id #uuid "e483500e-6827-5463-9cbf-bef05b8b48b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec1ebe33-0bb3-5ea6-a15e-48fad1ecf313" :question/math-academy-id "q-93686" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=6{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x},$ we have that $\\lambda =-3$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{-3x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-3$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (9{x}^{2}-12x+2){e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (9{x}^{2}-12x+2){e}^{-3x}+6(\\alpha (2x-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-3x})+9(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}) & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-12x+2+12x-18{x}^{2}+9{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bb4c6b6-40d9-5b70-85a1-5c0dd0ffdcf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404580} {:answer/id #uuid "8b330a7a-a2be-56f1-b818-07a864dbbad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404581} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa3a929-0e7a-54a9-9a07-07a5c343ac0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-4{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404582} {:answer/id #uuid "a526c43d-80e4-5437-9498-5253a344dede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404583} {:answer/id #uuid "7273b628-287b-5f2f-a133-298fc3ae16e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=4{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a526c43d-80e4-5437-9498-5253a344dede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404583} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fbc191d-df1d-571b-8c12-8b934cb5af35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bdcc65d-ace5-50fe-a859-cbc9e6149544" :question/math-academy-id "q-93687" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=4{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+Bx{e}^{-5x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+Bx{e}^{-5x},$ we have that $\\lambda =-5$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-5x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-5$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x-5{x}^{2}){e}^{-5x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (25{x}^{2}-20x+2){e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (25{x}^{2}-20x+2){e}^{-5x}+10(\\alpha (2x-5{x}^{2}){e}^{-5x})+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}) & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-20x+2+20x-50{x}^{2}+25{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "934ee56b-b088-5533-ad4c-e3032ce0c524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404587} {:answer/id #uuid "058adaab-a69e-5815-b046-5007fd02b2b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404588} {:answer/id #uuid "a209fd47-aee2-5925-bf14-e92cdd3c3c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404589} {:answer/id #uuid "36905ca1-84ae-504d-9cfb-76b204089ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404590} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2cc0e9-db58-596d-8e97-547728c8fbf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d2cc0e9-db58-596d-8e97-547728c8fbf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404591} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3a0d7ea-2403-5870-baf3-f1e3941ec6cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "311b05cd-8042-5d05-b094-868c3c494826" :question/math-academy-id "q-93785" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=3x+4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+2\\alpha +(\\alpha x+\\beta )=3x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n\\alpha x+(2\\alpha +\\beta )=3x+4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c1bc30d-5d97-531d-afdf-3b009ec8f6e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404594} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d9405d-2ce1-5d44-a565-736aab4014b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404595} {:answer/id #uuid "08f95f28-6f8e-5fcb-9731-71eae082edfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404596} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7af39c-7a3a-5f9f-bde9-57eb8955a31b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404597} {:answer/id #uuid "44d6e900-c60f-5e54-af5c-dd92a213dddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8d9405d-2ce1-5d44-a565-736aab4014b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404595} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3e0996f-4794-5687-809d-9b90cfff9300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c05c30a6-28a6-5452-ac44-e10a5f53cc02" :question/math-academy-id "q-93824" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+36y=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x,$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)+6x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =12(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)-36x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n12(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)-36x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)+36x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+12\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta =-12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x) \\\\ -12\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-1.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3f58c89-4322-5025-ad4e-44b4a9e319d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2{x}^{2}+4x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404601} {:answer/id #uuid "9902e94c-1538-5728-97b1-cc6ea9083789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404602} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0cf0b0-92d0-5bec-a856-b7e33785c711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404603} {:answer/id #uuid "c236eb3e-567c-5b28-8468-aa92c15044bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404604} {:answer/id #uuid "73507755-94b6-591e-897a-7a629dca8f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9902e94c-1538-5728-97b1-cc6ea9083789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404602} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae8a466-b526-5117-aeba-5c0fc28144b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f5f62bb-4839-53ca-a27c-2aa63221765a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93827" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c4037e5-607d-5cc4-9ce8-f7f1cd2c5aae" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 2D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5678c610-8c61-519a-ac89-a4ee573dbe85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and ${x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404607} {:answer/id #uuid "36171aeb-8b2d-55df-b514-cb0bb92c32d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404608} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecc1296-5d76-5959-b316-cb317bc507ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and ${x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404609} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a74df8-1742-5032-960e-2ec73e5f4a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and ${x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404610} {:answer/id #uuid "31e9810b-6835-5b51-bb36-14e635b7db32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31e9810b-6835-5b51-bb36-14e635b7db32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404611} :answer-field/id #uuid "c651d92a-608f-50d0-a05c-565c2587225b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca11e17a-4464-5c54-bbb1-cb2d8dc175df" :question/math-academy-id "q-93838" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n2{y}_{1}^{2}-\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} & =2([{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}]-{x}_{2}^{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})-{x}_{2}^{2} & =2({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $3\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ebd4f6a-e711-5a30-817f-632a3798bec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404613} {:answer/id #uuid "fa61a26f-ad52-5dd6-9a0a-58b7b29605f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404614} {:answer/id #uuid "df920d21-8207-5b52-a72a-8fc3592e0c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404615} {:answer/id #uuid "cb49a1af-87c1-5d9f-814f-b3600e3126c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404616} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd96fc6-a365-548a-a677-09d28338ad35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df920d21-8207-5b52-a72a-8fc3592e0c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404615} :answer-field/id #uuid "a620906d-6f92-5060-b907-57c31554da32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b755a996-db20-51e4-9f08-2f14dd2e4212" :question/math-academy-id "q-93952" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. $(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0$\nII. $8{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=10$\nIII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+1,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b137cc-58d8-54b3-8432-760f0c24ad5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404620} {:answer/id #uuid "8a282dc6-f613-5c49-b5b1-97ae8b91871d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404621} {:answer/id #uuid "ea78a723-5fcb-5124-a34d-7c785209ed96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404622} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8a0614-8f54-566a-a177-8dffc8e8215a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404623} {:answer/id #uuid "214fedb5-e346-584d-9ffc-111bf69f0cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "214fedb5-e346-584d-9ffc-111bf69f0cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404624} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7b983f-90c9-5dfc-9769-8b7c27a8d13e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "976b5be4-8521-548d-a6b3-f77ad28ed1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-94041" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c224e271-277d-5f1a-9cc2-c5f3c1736629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & -\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404627} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e4a6c1-e440-5739-9e5b-4073535092ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404628} {:answer/id #uuid "51ae4b95-