 matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83034cba-0c66-5f4a-b7ab-09a5c1a7fcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404633} {:answer/id #uuid "febd8376-fe28-5c27-8963-ff102a571340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404634} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6af10c-5fa3-59e2-8df1-756aa2dce145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404635} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5de280-13f5-504f-9445-8833d1c988ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404636} {:answer/id #uuid "69324513-aecc-5af8-be4a-19ff3184bb08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83034cba-0c66-5f4a-b7ab-09a5c1a7fcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404633} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f41eb2e-7fb3-52bc-a716-8a62499f8e67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f738fa7a-a5ce-518d-9ada-841b441fb202" :question/math-academy-id "q-94056" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e0b4012-403b-5739-8f33-3c79111129fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Elliptic Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332e3fdd-c5ab-5567-a7df-8d90823d6a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404639} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe302de-3bb5-5c7a-b989-c8e19404a6c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404640} {:answer/id #uuid "67a0f9e2-02bc-5268-8186-7f072105781d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404641} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1fac1f-d4fb-5122-bceb-8b8e8ae46732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404642} {:answer/id #uuid "73cf50ce-277e-5c94-9a9d-dfc2fc91bcef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67a0f9e2-02bc-5268-8186-7f072105781d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404641} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1c20ea0-f41d-5667-aeb9-8dd4fb8d87da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0cabaaa-77b2-5df5-b276-82aad1e2675a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94081" :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=(z+3)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{4} & =(0+3)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{4}{4} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+1 & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-2,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c4037e5-607d-5cc4-9ce8-f7f1cd2c5aae" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Form to Canonical Form Using Lagrange's Method: 2D-Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5218bd-78ef-5fa2-b2a7-66efde9a8122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404646} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3f10df-fdfa-56b7-a1de-da199a72f22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404647} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6c1183-9409-57f2-97d5-ddf9a03ba804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and ${x}_{2}-{x}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404648} {:answer/id #uuid "0e9c2ae1-d2dd-58b7-a6a3-cfd240449ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404649} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f175cf-21e5-5c59-a3e3-5498ae74ba7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5218bd-78ef-5fa2-b2a7-66efde9a8122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404646} :answer-field/id #uuid "34930159-fd19-5a7b-abd1-198173eae884" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "106b083d-c143-5f48-8526-3cd804d072e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94139" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+7{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2} & =9([{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}]-{x}_{2}^{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+16{x}_{2}^{2} & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}+7{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n9{y}_{1}^{2}+7{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $9\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}.$"}]}
